SUMMARY:Dear Darling Grand Opening: A Third Space\, Kitchen\, & Bar
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260614T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260614T230000
UID:blogto-234651@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/05/05/original_image070903bd-3310-40be-a37c-a38d10c3c2
 18.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Richmond St is getting the third space Toronto has been asking
  for.. Dear Darling opens May 14- a kitchen\, bar\, and third space with r
 otating events weekly\, including saxophone & jazz nights\, weekend DJs\, 
 community events\, & vibes for people who want to be a regular somewhere\,
  but just haven't found a spot they click with yet.\nThe concept is simple
 : good cocktails\, a kitchen that actually complements the drinks (and doe
 sn't cost half a months rent)\, and enough going on events-wise that you'l
 l have a reason to come back every few weeks (or a few times a week.) It's
  the kind of place that works for a date night just as well as a night out
  with friends\, and will be the full package once the patio is open for br
 unch come this summer.\nCheck out @deardarling.to on Instagram for more in
 fo.
LOCATION:Dear Darling
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/dear-darling-grand-opening-a-third-space
 -kitchen-bar-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Indigenous Art Journey
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260614T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260614T160000
UID:blogto-234454@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/04/30/original_image529bdbe4-ff21-473a-80d1-d0a72f43c5
 8c.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Indigenous Art Journey Returns to Toronto with Free Community 
 Event Celebrating Indigenous Artists\nToronto\, ON — Shawish Market is p
 roud to present the Indigenous Art Journey\, a free\, one-day community ev
 ent celebrating the beauty\, resilience\, and diversity of Indigenous arti
 stry for Indigenous History Month. Taking place on Sunday\, June 14\, 2026
 \, from 10:00 AM to 4:00 PM at the Toronto Reference Library\, the event w
 ill bring together over 20 Indigenous artists for a day of art\, culture\,
  and connection.\nDesigned to uplift Indigenous creators and provide acces
 sible cultural experiences for the public\, Indigenous Art Journey will fe
 ature a curated art exhibit\, a vendor marketplace\, and interactive works
 hops led by Indigenous artists. Admission is free\, with optional donation
 s supporting Shawish Market’s mission to maintain a zero-fee platform fo
 r Indigenous vendors.\n“Shawish Market was created to remove barriers fo
 r Indigenous artists\, and Indigenous Art Journey brings that vision into 
 a physical space where community can connect directly with creators and th
 eir stories\,” says Amanda Bernard\, Founder of Shawish Market. “We in
 tentionally designed this event to be free and accessible\, because experi
 encing Indigenous art and culture shouldn’t be limited by cost. Everyone
  is welcome to come\, learn\, and connect.”\nEvent Highlights:\n1. Curat
 ed Indigenous Art Exhibit showcasing works from emerging and established a
 rtists\n2. Vendor Marketplace featuring over 20 Indigenous-owned brands\n3
 . Beading Workshops led by renowned artists Joy Rogers and Saga Kwandibenz
 \n4. Free Coffee\, Tea & Pastries available to attendees throughout the da
 y\nThe Indigenous Art Journey reflects a growing movement toward ethical c
 onsumption and cultural appreciation\, offering attendees an opportunity t
 o engage directly with artists\, learn traditional practices\, and support
  authentic Indigenous businesses. Both Indigenous and non-Indigenous parti
 cipants are welcome.\nThis event builds on Shawish Market’s broader miss
 ion to empower Indigenous entrepreneurs across Turtle Island by eliminatin
 g vendor fees and fostering sustainable economic growth within communities
 .\nFor more information\, event details\, or to reserve a spot\, visit:  h
 ttps://www.eventbrite.ca/e/indigenous-art-journey-presented-by-shawish-mar
 ket-tickets-1983491297866?aff=oddtdtcreator\nMedia Contact:\nAmanda Bernar
 d\nFounder\, Shawish Market\nshawish.market@gmail.com\nwww.shawishmarket.c
 om  / Instagram: @shawish.market
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/indigenous-art-journey-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Introduction to Natural Dyeing
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260614T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260614T160000
UID:blogto-234746@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/05/07/original_imagee07815ea-5d30-403a-b987-d9e37fb2b5
 e6.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Join us in creating fabric samples from plants in this 3-hour 
 workshop! Participants will be guided through various natural dye methods\
 , including bundle dyeing\, flower pounding and vat dyeing using natural d
 yes. Each participant will leave with 3 unique samples on fabric\, and a d
 eeper appreciation for sustainability and plant matter!\n\nBeginners welco
 me. All materials supplied. PPE will be available (masks and gloves).
LOCATION:Toronto Botanical Garden
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/introduction-to-natural-dyeing-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:John Lilly and the Earth Coincidence Control Office
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260614T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260614T143700
UID:blogto-235041@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/05/14/original_image742a96c3-d467-4fbe-88a6-05632cd846
 62.jpg
DESCRIPTION:“One of the most consistently inventive\, impossible-to-cate
 gorize directors of our time\, Almereyda has shown a fascination with visi
 onary characters across both his narrative and documentary films” – Th
 e Criterion Channel\nThe daring experimenter Dr. John C. Lilly dedicated h
 is life to radical self-investigation and unlocking the mysteries of consc
 iousness and communication. “My body is my laboratory” was the motto\,
  and his research on the language of dolphins and whales—as well as psyc
 hedelics and sensory deprivation—assured his own cult status in 20th-cen
 tury pop culture as the basis for Ken Russell’s Altered States and Mike 
 Nichols’s The Day of the Dolphin.\nDirectors Michael Almareyda and Court
 ney Stephens\, along with narrator Chloë Sevigny\, explore the life of a 
 determined scientist and his experiments into the psychonautical unknown.
LOCATION:Hot Docs Cinema
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/john-lilly-and-the-earth-coincidence-con
 trol-office-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
