BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:I Am Trying to Break Your Heart: A Film About Wilco
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260618T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260618T204500
UID:blogto-235149@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/05/18/original_image34691623-b97f-4f59-ae1a-bd596c7a12
 47.jpg
DESCRIPTION:“Functions as both a revealing look at the collaborative pro
 cess and a timely\, tongue-in-cheek profile of the corporate circus that i
 s the recording industry.” — The Hollywood Reporter\n"An exciting and 
 involving rock music doc\, a smart and satisfying look inside that tumultu
 ous world.” — Los Angeles Times\n“One of the greatest music document
 aries ever made.” — Rolling Stone\nBuilding on three critically acclai
 med records and a reputation for phenomenal live shows\, Wilco seemed pois
 ed to cement its reputation as one of the great American rock groups. So h
 ow is it that one year later\, with completed record in hand\, the band fo
 und itself rejected by its corporate label and missing two of its original
  members? First-time filmmaker and award-winning photographer Sam Jones wa
 s on hand\, chronicling this turbulent chapter in Wilco's history as it un
 folded.\nThroughout the film\, Jones tempers the backstage dramas and unfa
 thomable corporate shenanigans with inspired live performances\, as frontm
 an Jeff Tweedy and company perform songs from Yankee Hotel Foxtrot and pre
 vious Wilco albums. Shot in luminous black and white\, I Am Trying to Brea
 k Your Heart is a riveting portrait of a band making the best music of its
  career.\nJoin producer Peter Abraham for a special post-screening Q&A via
  Zoom.
LOCATION:Hot Docs Cinema
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/i-am-trying-to-break-your-heart-a-film-a
 bout-wilco-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Ingredients for Queer Food and the Makings of a Feminist Restauran
 t
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260618T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260618T210000
UID:blogto-235212@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/05/19/original_imageee2e8487-21cb-45ee-ae0d-9c84c2acbf
 18.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Coinciding with the 50th anniversary of the trailblazing resta
 urant Mother Courage of New York City\, Alex Ketchum's book Ingredients fo
 r Revolution: A History of American Feminist Restaurants\,Cafes\, and Coff
 eehouses is the first history of the more than 230 feminist and lesbian-fe
 minist restaurants\, cafes\, and coffeehouses that existed from 1972 to th
 e present.\nIn celebration of Pride Month\, the Culinary Historians of Can
 ada has invited Dr. Ketchum to discuss how these Canadian and American eat
 eries were and continue to be key sites of cultural and political signific
 ance. These institutions served an essential role for multiple social just
 ice movements including women’s liberation\, LGBTQ equality\, and food j
 ustice\, as well as for training women workers and entrepreneurs.\nOnline 
 Webinar. Tickets start at $17.31
LOCATION:Virtual Event
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/ingredients-for-queer-food-and-the-makin
 gs-of-a-feminist-restaurant-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:John Simone's Photographic Voyages - 300 photographs - 50 Countrie
 s - Five Continents
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260618T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260618T190000
UID:blogto-235272@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/05/20/original_imagef33ecea3-8ef6-431c-95d1-762743c1f6
 d0.jpg
DESCRIPTION:John Simone’s Photographic Voyages is a major photography ex
 hibition presented during Nuit Rose Festival 2026 at Toronto’s Youngplac
 e Arts Centre.\nThe exhibition features 300 photographs captured across 50
  countries and five continents\, exploring shared humanity\, culture\, arc
 hitecture\, ritual\, performance\, spirituality\, humour\, street life\, a
 nd everyday moments from around the world. Drawn from years of internation
 al travel and documentary photography\, the exhibition transforms the gall
 ery into an immersive visual journey through global observation and storyt
 elling.\nJohn Simone first became known for documenting New York nightlife
  and celebrity culture during the late 1980s\, and later spent five years 
 travelling internationally as Senior Photographer and Photography Teacher 
 aboard Princess Cruises.\n“I’m not interested in capturing the world\,
 ” says Simone. “I’m interested in catching it in the act.”\nSimone
 ’s work has appeared in exhibitions including The Last Party: Nightworld
  in Photographs at the Serge Sorokko Gallery in San Francisco\, Subterrane
 an Society in New York City\, and previous exhibitions at Nuit Rose in Tor
 onto.\nTimed to coincide with Toronto’s international summer cultural se
 ason and increased global tourism during the FIFA World Cup\, John Simone
 ’s Photographic Voyages presents journeys through street life\, remote l
 andscapes\, architecture\, performance\, spirituality\, and human connecti
 on across five continents.\n“I’m not interested in capturing the world
 \,” says Simone. “I’m interested in catching it in the act.”\n“S
 imone’s quirky lens turns itself toward the peculiar theatre of the worl
 d — finding beauty\, absurdity\, ritual\, and humanity in equal measure.
 ”\n— Kelly McCray\, Curatorial Framework\nExhibition Dates:\nThursday\
 , June 18 – Sunday\, July 5\, 2026\nVenue:\nYoungplace Arts Centre\nThir
 d Floor Gallery — North & South\n180 Shaw Street\, Toronto\nGallery Hour
 s:\nMonday–Friday: 9AM – 7PM\nSaturday–Sunday: 9AM – 5PM\nOpening 
 Reception:\nThursday\, June 18\, 2026 — 7PM\nArtist Attendance:\nSundays
  — June 21\, June 28 & July 5\, 2026\n1PM – 4PM\nAdmission:\nFree\nWeb
 site:\nhttps://johnsimonephotography.com/voyages.html
LOCATION:Youngplace Arts Centre
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/john-simones-photographic-voyages-300-ph
 otographs-50-countries-five-continents-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Los Callejeros Exhibition Reception
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260618T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260618T200000
UID:blogto-228596@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2025/12/29/original_image5d9c7202-d7c7-486a-885e-b81b0750d5
 85.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Explore selections from works reflecting Jim Bravo’s memorie
 s of his early immigrant inner-city experiences\, and correlated interpret
 ations of our sublime Canadian landscape. This exhibition adds yet another
  voice to the developing story and renewed global interest surrounding que
 stions of race and immigration witnessed in society recently.\nAbout the A
 rtist: Jim Bravo was born in Ecuador\, South America\, and is of Afro-Lati
 no and Quichua descent. He emigrated to Toronto Canada with his family in 
 the mid 70s and spent his youth in the downtown central west and east core
  of the city. He is a graduate of The Ontario College of Art and Design Un
 iversity where he majored in Fine Art and public monumental painting. A mu
 lti award winning painter including first honor for the prestigious 2023 K
 ingston Prize For Contemporary Canadian Portraiture\, Bravo is also the re
 cipient of grants from Toronto Art Council\, Ontario Arts Council grants a
 nd TABIA Mural Award.\nExhibit is open from June 18\, 2026 until July 23\,
  2026\nEvery Monday to Friday at 10 a.m. - 5 p.m.\nEvery Saturday at 10 a.
 m. - 1 p.m.\nOpening reception: June 18\, 6 p.m. - 8 p.m.\nGallery is clos
 ed on Sundays and statutory holidays.
LOCATION:Assembly Hall
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/los-callejeros-exhibition-reception-toro
 nto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:National Ballet of Canada: Maddaddam
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260618T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260618T213000
UID:blogto-228660@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2025/12/31/original_image079c2a97-f134-48a5-956b-6df13e9dce
 96.jpg
DESCRIPTION:To experience MADDADDAM is to be immersed in the imagination a
 nd unflinching moral acuity of Canada’s Margaret Atwood on subjects of r
 ampant technological experimentation\, colonialism and greed. Wayne McGreg
 or’s 2022 co-production with The Royal Ballet brings Atwood’s post-apo
 calyptic MaddAddam trilogy to life\, exploring themes of extinction and in
 vention\, hubris and humanity\, spliced together with Atwood’s signature
  activism and connection to Canada. Scored by one of the greatest living c
 omposers\, Max Richter\, MADDADDAM is an unforgettable production for our 
 time.\nTickets available @ https://eventschaser.com/?s=Maddaddam+Toronto
LOCATION:Four Seasons Centre
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/national-ballet-of-canada-maddaddam-toro
 nto-10/
