BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Shades of Feeling: Five by Pratibha Parmar - with Saffron Maeve
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260621T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260621T145000
UID:blogto-235045@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/05/14/original_image0e8f7412-09eb-4d32-811a-333b9fb173
 9b.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Toronto-based critic\, editor\, and film curator Saffron Maeve
  shares a collection of five shorts by British filmmaker Pratibha Parmar\,
  released from 1988-1991.\n"Pratibha’s works have a strong decolonial se
 nsibility that I immediately respond to\, and I admire her ability to be a
 t once playful\, coy\, headstrong\, and critical in her depictions of quee
 r South Asian diasporas. She understands the shared urgency of desire and 
 the constraints that are placed upon it." – Saffron Maeve on Shades of F
 eeling: Five by Pratibha Parmar\nFrom the 80s into the 90s\, British filmm
 aker Pratibha Parmar established a body of short films and video poems dea
 ling intimately with ethnic and sexual expressivity in a blinkered UK. Car
 ving out a queer South Asian imaginary\, Parmar’s films prioritize pleas
 ure (khush) and thematize lesbian chat rooms\, the infiltration of colonia
 l architecture\, desire and diaspora\, and the writings of Indian lesbian 
 poet Suniti Bamjoshi. Parmar’s spatially engaged strategies of revolt ar
 e both protective and generous\, clarifying the importance of anti-colonia
 l perspectives on queerness. This program originated at the Brooklyn Acade
 my of Music in partnership with Alfreda’s Cinema.\nShades of Feeling: Fi
 ve by Pratibha Parmar will be presented with a live introduction from Saff
 ron Maeve and a special pre-recorded Q&A with director Pratibha Parmar.\nS
 affron Maeve is a Toronto-based critic\, editor\, and film curator. She is
  a regular contributor to The Globe and Mail\, and her writing has appeare
 d in Film Comment\, Sight and Sound\, Reverse Shot\, MUBI Notebook\, Docum
 entary Magazine\, and more. She is the curator of the screening series CON
 TOURS at Paradise Theatre and Apparatus Theory at Revue Cinema\, and the q
 uarterly reading series Crit Salon. Saffron has curated programs for TIFF\
 , the Brooklyn Academy of Music\, The Cinematheque (BC)\, and Spectacle Th
 eater. Presently\, Saffron is pursuing a PhD in Cinema & Media Studies at 
 York University.
LOCATION:Hot Docs Cinema
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/shades-of-feeling-five-by-pratibha-parma
 r-with-saffron-maeve-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
