BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Christopher Mellon\, former Deputy Assistant Secretary of Defense 
 for Intelligence\, to Keynote 2026 SCU Annual Conference on Unidentified A
 nomalous Phenomena in Toronto
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260724T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260724T180000
UID:blogto-234557@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/05/04/original_imaged5310b25-2fdd-482c-8f02-51f0008d67
 29.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Mellon\, former Deputy Assistant Secretary of Defe
 nse for Intelligence and former Minority Staff Director of the U.S. Senate
  Select Committee on Intelligence\, will headline the 2026 Scientific Coal
 ition for UAP Studies (SCU) Annual Conference\, taking place July 24–26\
 , 2026\, at the Marriott Eaton Centre in Toronto. The theme of the confere
 nce is “The Role of Science and Global Governments in UAP Research.”\n
 Mellon’s keynote address\, “Science\, National Security\, and UAP\,”
  will anchor a three-day international gathering of scientists\, researche
 rs\, and policy experts examining Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAP) t
 hrough rigorous\, multidisciplinary approaches.\nHosted by SCU\, a U.S.-ba
 sed organization with global membership\, the conference will bring togeth
 er leading voices from Canada\, the United States\, Japan\, France\, Germa
 ny\, Hungary\, and beyond. Participants will explore UAP research across f
 ields\, including physics and astronomy\, neurology\, anthropology\, artif
 icial intelligence\, and data science.\nFeatured speakers include:\nBenjam
 in Fields\, BA\, astronomy and astrophysics\, U.S.\nUAP in a SETI Context:
  Constructing Astrobiologically Plausible Origin Scenarios for E.T. Visita
 tion\nChristian Peters\, PhD\, Managing Director\, Bremen International Gr
 aduate School of Social Sciences\, University of Bremen\, Germany\nBetween
  Epistemic Ideals and Political Realities: Rethinking the Role of Science 
 in Governmental UAP Research\nChris Rutkowski\, BSc\, MEd\, science journa
 list\, research coordinator of the Canadian UFO Survey\, Manitoba\, CA\nCa
 nadian Government Studies on UAP: Sky Canada\, UTIAS\, and More\nProf. Dou
 g Buettner.\, physics\, Univ. of Utah\, and Deputy Chief Scientist at the 
 Stevens Institute of Technology\, AIRC\, U.S.\, SCU Board member\nRevisiti
 ng the Aguadilla Trans-medium UAP\nGergo Szoboszlai\, MD candidate\, Semme
 lweis University\, Hungary\nNeurological and Health Effects Associated wit
 h Human Encounters with Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAP)\nGlen A. Ro
 bertson\, MS\, physics and mathematics\, NASA-MSFC Retired\, U.S.\nWarp Dr
 ive from Quantum Field Energy\nProf. Kevin Knuth\, physics\, University at
  Albany\, U.S.\nUnidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAP): Energy and Power 
 — How Anomalous is Anomalous?\nProf. Laura Dominé.\, physics\, Natl. In
 stitute of Information and Communication Technology (NICT) and Kyoto Unive
 rsity\, Japan\, SCU Board Member\nAnalysis of Historical UAP Reports in Ja
 pan and Critical Evaluation of Studies Alleging Evidence for Technosignatu
 res in the POSS1-E Photographic Plates\nProf. Matthew Szydagis\, physics\,
  University at Albany\, U.S.\nUAPx Reborn Within the Academy\nMichael Vail
 lant\, M.Sc.\, data scientist\, formerly at the National Centre for Space 
 Studies’ GEIPAN\, France\nLessons from the GEIPAN Experience: Science\, 
 UAP Investigation\, and the Case for an International Standards Coalition\
 nMaya Cowan\, PhD candidate\, anthropology\, Binghamton University\, U.S.\
 nObserving the Unknown: Emerging Cultures of Expertise in UAP Science\nMik
 e Cifone\, PhD\, philosophy\, Pres./ED\, Society for UAP Studies\, U.S.\nB
 uilding a Science of UAP: Institutional Design\, Policy\, and Scientific L
 egitimacy\nRainer Haseitl\, BSc\, data acquisition and software\, Germany\
 , CIO Hessdalen Project\, Norway\nInvitation to Hessdalen: An Active Hotsp
 ot for Instrumental UAP Research\nRandy Bostick\, PhD\, physics\, former S
 cience Advisor\, AARO\, U.S.\nApplying Scientific Rigor to UAP Study\nProf
 . Richard Griffiths\, physics and astronomy\, University of Hawaii\, U.S.\
 nCan we use the equivalent of ‘gravitational lensing’ to verify or con
 strain the use of spacetime bubbles for the motion of extraterrestrial spa
 cecraft?\nRobert W. McGwier\, PhD\, Cohere Technology Group\, U.S.\nMulti-
 Modal Autonomous Sensing: A Scientifically Credible Framework for Persiste
 nt UAP and USO Detection\nSean Grosvenor\, BA\, crime scene investigation 
 field supervisor\, retired\, U.S.\nSCU Investigations\nProf. Stephen Brueh
 l\, anesthesiology\, Vanderbilt University School of Medicine\, U.S. Assoc
 iations Between Transient Star-Like Objects\, Nuclear Testing\, and UAP: T
 he Good\, The Bad\, and The Ugly of Peer-Reviewed UAP Science\nSCU's goal 
 for the conference is to raise awareness of UAP research as interest in it
  continues to grow across scientific and governmental communities worldwid
 e. The organization is dedicated to advancing transparency\, data integrit
 y\, and cross-disciplinary collaboration in advancing the field of UAP res
 earch.\nThe conference will also offer opportunities for students\, media\
 , government (former and active)\, civilian researchers\, and academics to
  engage directly with experts and participate in discussions shaping the f
 uture of UAP science.\nAbout SCU\nThe Scientific Coalition for UAP Studies
  (SCU) is a nonprofit interdisciplinary research organization dedicated to
  the scientific investigation of Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAP). C
 omposed of scientists\, engineers\, former military and intelligence profe
 ssionals\, and law enforcement experts\, SCU applies rigorous analytical m
 ethods and advanced tools to evaluate aerial and anomalous phenomena. SCU 
 collaborates with government agencies\, academic institutions\, and privat
 e sector partners to ensure objective\, evidence-based analysis\, enhance 
 reporting systems\, and provide actionable insights to improve public safe
 ty and airspace security. Its research supports transparency\, responsible
  evaluation\, and the advancement of scientific understanding of phenomena
  that are not readily explained by conventional technologies.\nFor more in
 formation\, including registration\, tickets\, schedule details\, and acco
 mmodations\, visit ExploreSCU.org or follow updates on X at @ExploreSCU.
LOCATION:Marriott Eaton Centre
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/christopher-mellon-former-deputy-assista
 nt-secretary-of-defense-for-intelligence-to-keynote-2026-scu-annual-confer
 ence-on-unidentified-anomalous-phenomena-in-toronto-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
